  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Complex-Valued Fourier Coefficients",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute complex-valued Fourier series coefficients by evaluating the one-period complex-exponential coefficient integral and interpreting each result as the harmonic's amplitude-and-phase weight.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Complex-Valued Fourier Coefficients",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Complex-Valued Fourier Coefficients",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A complex Fourier series writes a periodic signal as $x(t)=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty} c_k e^{jk\\omega_0 t}$. The index $k$ labels the harmonic, and $\\omega_0$ is the fundamental angular frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty} c_k e^{jk\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To find one coefficient, multiply by the matching exponential $e^{-jk\\omega_0 t}$ and average over one period $T$. That one-period projection isolates the $k$th harmonic and gives its complex weight $c_k$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_k=\\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T x(t)e^{-jk\\omega_0 t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with a constant signal: only the $k=0$ term survives, so $c_0$ is the DC coefficient. Then look at a single cosine harmonic at the fundamental frequency; the same projection rule isolates that harmonic before any mixed signal adds extra bookkeeping.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "You can read each coefficient as $c_k=|c_k|e^{j\\angle c_k}$. The magnitude tells you how strong the harmonic is, and the angle tells you its phase shift.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_k=|c_k|e^{j\\angle c_k}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Complex-Valued Fourier Coefficients",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Evaluating One Complex Coefficient Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=4\\cos(\\omega_0 t-\\pi/3)$, compute $c_1$ from $c_1=\\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T x(t)e^{-j\\omega_0 t}\\,dt$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue here is a single shifted cosine at the fundamental frequency, so the coefficient integral should isolate the matching $k=1$ harmonic. Rewrite the cosine in complex-exponential form first, then multiply by the matching exponential $e^{-j\\omega_0 t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{4}{2}\\left(e^{j(\\omega_0 t-\\pi/3)}+e^{-j(\\omega_0 t-\\pi/3)}\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "After multiplication by $e^{-j\\omega_0 t}$, the $e^{j\\omega_0 t}$ term becomes constant, while the other term becomes a $-2\\omega_0$ exponential that averages to zero over one period. That is the orthogonality step that leaves only the matching harmonic behind.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nc_1 &= \\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T 4\\cos(\\omega_0 t-\\pi/3)e^{-j\\omega_0 t}\\,dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T 2\\left(e^{j(\\omega_0 t-\\pi/3)}+e^{-j(\\omega_0 t-\\pi/3)}\\right)e^{-j\\omega_0 t}\\,dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T 2\\left(e^{-j\\pi/3}+e^{-j(2\\omega_0 t-\\pi/3)}\\right)\\,dt \\\\\n&= 2e^{-j\\pi/3}\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the integral isolates the $k=1$ harmonic and returns $c_1=2e^{-j\\pi/3}$. That coefficient is the complex weight of the fundamental cosine term: its magnitude is $2$, and its angle is $-\\pi/3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Complex-Valued Fourier Coefficients",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Simplifying a Coefficient into Rectangular Form",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Starting from $c_1=2e^{-j\\pi/3}$, simplify the coefficient into rectangular form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The only new wrinkle is the representation change. Use Euler's formula to rewrite the coefficient as a real part plus an imaginary part, then keep the magnitude $2$ outside the trigonometric terms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_1=2\\bigl(\\cos(-\\pi/3)+j\\sin(-\\pi/3)\\bigr)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute $\\cos(-\\pi/3)=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin(-\\pi/3)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. That turns the exponential coefficient into a rectangular expression with the same harmonic weight.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nc_1 &= 2\\bigl(\\cos(-\\pi/3)+j\\sin(-\\pi/3)\\bigr) \\\\\n&= 2\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-j\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\\n&= 1-j\\sqrt{3}\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the coefficient in rectangular form is $c_1=1-j\\sqrt{3}$. It is the same $k=1$ harmonic weight as before, just written as $a+jb$ instead of exponential form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Complex-Valued Fourier Coefficients",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Converting a Complex Coefficient to Magnitude and Phase",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $c_1=1-j\\sqrt{3}$, convert it to magnitude and phase.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the rectangular coefficient $c_1=1-j\\sqrt{3}$. The task is to read off its magnitude and its angle, because that polar pair is the amplitude-and-phase weight of the $k=1$ harmonic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|c_1|=\\sqrt{1^2+(-\\sqrt{3})^2}=\\sqrt{1+3}=2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The real part is positive and the imaginary part is negative, so $c_1$ lies in quadrant IV. That means the phase is negative, with reference angle $\\pi/3$ from the right triangle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\\angle c_1&=-\\pi/3\\\\c_1&=2e^{-j\\pi/3}\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the same harmonic weight is now written as magnitude $2$ with phase $-\\pi/3$. This is the form used when you want to see how strongly the harmonic appears and what phase shift it carries.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Complex-Valued Fourier Coefficients",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Positive and Negative Harmonic Weights",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For a real periodic signal with $c_2=1-j$ and $c_{-2}=1+j$, interpret what the positive and negative harmonic weights mean in the reconstruction.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the signal is real, the coefficients at $+2$ and $-2$ must be conjugates. The cue is the pair, and the rule is that one real harmonic stores its amplitude-and-phase weight across both signs.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}c_2&=1-j=\\sqrt{2}e^{-j\\pi/4}\\\\c_{-2}&=1+j=\\sqrt{2}e^{j\\pi/4}=c_2^*\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use that pair inside the synthesis sum. The two complex exponentials combine into one real oscillation, so they are not two separate harmonics.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}c_2e^{j2\\omega_0 t}+c_{-2}e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}&=(1-j)e^{j2\\omega_0 t}+(1+j)e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}\\\\&=2\\sqrt{2}\\cos(2\\omega_0 t-\\pi/4)\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the positive-index coefficient carries the amplitude-phase information: magnitude $\\sqrt{2}$ and phase $-\\pi/4$. The negative-index coefficient is the conjugate partner that makes the reconstruction real, and together they represent one $k=2$ harmonic with amplitude $2\\sqrt{2}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
